  {
    "episode_id": 95591,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95591.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Why do you think it is that so many people on the right are so quick to conflate antitrust solutions with regulatory solutions? Because it seems to me that those are actually in opposition on a fundamental level.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Many people on the right quickly conflate antitrust solutions with regulatory solutions.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Antitrust solutions and regulatory solutions are fundamentally opposed to each other.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that distinguishing between antitrust and regulation is important for meaningful policy discourse.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker assumes that conflating antitrust and regulation reflects a misunderstanding of their core functions.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views the right-wing political perspective as systematically prone to conceptual confusion on economic policy issues.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker considers 'fundamental' opposition between antitrust and regulation to be analytically valid and widely applicable.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker trusts their own understanding of economic institutions enough to critique others’ framing of them.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 95591,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95591.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "I think you're right, they are in opposition, and what we've seen the left try to do, and this dates back to the progressive areas, basically get out of antitrust in favor of government regulation, heavy oversight, and that change really started to happen in a big way in the Wilson administration to take us all the way back a century, and that's basically been the left's the left's approach. I mean, you see them flirt a little bit with antitrust. Franklin Roosevelt flirted a little bit with antitrust briefly, but not really in any sustained fashion. The left generally likes big government and big business. And the great thing about big business from their point of view is they can extort it. You know, they can threaten big business and say, now, if you don't do this, then we're going to put regulations on you to do that. But now, as you know, big business in the left are big-time allies. I mean, who's the biggest pusher of the woke agenda? It's the woke capitalists. It's the woke corporations. I mean, who is pushing left-wing social change in America? It's woke business interests. who are in league with big government. So there's no accident about that. That's not happenstance. And so I think this takes us back to, rather than trying to layer on regulation after regulation, multiply regulatory agencies, all that kind of make deals with these companies. No, just let's just get back to competition. And by the way, let's see if we can't encourage some new small businesses and some independent producers along the way and get them into our economy and deconcentrate it so that the woke capitalists don't have all of this power. So there's a rich anti-monopoly tradition that goes all the way back to our framers, and it certainly was a big part of our Republican past that I think is worth recovering.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Competition should replace layers of regulation and agency deals with corporations.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The left has historically favored government regulation over antitrust enforcement.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The alignment between woke corporations and big government is intentional, not accidental.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The shift toward regulatory preference began during the Wilson administration.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Encouraging small businesses and independent producers would help reduce corporate concentration.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Franklin Roosevelt briefly considered antitrust but did not pursue it seriously.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The left generally supports both big government and big business.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Big business can be coerced through threats of regulation.",
