        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Despite Republican control in 2016, meaningful policy changes did not occur regarding big tech.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Republican lawmakers' actions after hearings consist mostly of sending letters and complaints.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The typical response from Republicans to criticism is to encourage voters to elect more Republicans.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Major technology companies have vast influence comparable to nation-states.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Effective political action requires more than symbolic gestures like public hearings and complaints.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Electoral success alone is insufficient to enact substantive regulatory change on big tech.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Big tech companies operate with de facto autonomy due to their scale and economic power.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Audiences value concrete policy solutions over performative accountability measures.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Mark Zuckerberg represents a central figure in the power structure of big tech that warrants scrutiny.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 95591,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95591.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think that, you know, the only thing that will change that is voters saying over and over and unequivocally that you've got to do better than that. You know, I could say as a pro-life conservative that we've in the pro-life movement, we've struggled with that for years where you've got Republicans who will run all the time, pretty much every Republican will run and say, oh, I'm absolutely pro-life, 100%. You bet you could count on me and then you get, they get to Washington and it's like they don't want to take any votes on it. They don't want to go out in favor of pro-life justices. They don't want to put their their names on the line, and I just think that that unfortunately is a pattern. But hey, at the end of the day, what I've learned in my short two years in the Senate is, if there's anything that concentrates the mind of an elected official, it's the will of voters. And I think what you're seeing from our voters is, and this is why so many Senate Republicans, I think, have changed their tune on Big Tech, it's because their voters at home are saying, we're not gonna stand for it. We're not gonna stand for being censored in our own country. We're not going to stand for our children being exploited in our own homes. We're not going to have it. And I think that you're starting to see a Republican shift. So what we need now is good policy. You know, we've got to say, okay, what are we going to do about it? And this is why I come back to the number one thing we've got to do is we've got to break these companies up. We've got to break up their structural power, not just the tech companies, but the monopolies, the woke capitalists, the monopolists across the economy. And then when it comes to big tech, we've got a lot of people to sue them. I mean, you know, if you get de-platformed, if you're censored in violation of these tech companies own terms of service, because they all say, oh, we don't censor, we don't do that. Well, fine. Make that enforceable. So if somebody gets censored, if you get de-platformed, if you get taken down, you can sue and have your day in court. So I think you do that and you let people, and you break them up.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The only factor capable of changing political behavior among elected officials is repeated, clear voter demands.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Voters' consistent and unambiguous expressions of dissatisfaction are necessary to compel action from politicians.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Elected officials respond primarily to electoral pressure rather than moral conviction or institutional norms.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Voter influence is more effective than internal party dynamics or ideological alignment in shaping legislative priorities.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "There exists a gap between campaign rhetoric and actual policy implementation by Republican politicians on pro-life issues.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Political accountability is contingent on visible public demand rather than abstract principles or advocacy.",
