  {
    "episode_id": 95591,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95591.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "One policy where there's certainly a good bit of conversation, but not necessarily a good bit of agreement is the question around childcare in America. Obviously, Senator Romney has a proposal on childcare. There are other proposals that have been floated out there as well. But I think one of the concerns for a lot of conservative traditionalists is that by going down that route, you are ultimately sending the message that women really ought to get out of the home and to go to work, and that you want to have a family that has two earners. as opposed to one that has one. What's your view of the prioritization that ought to go in that direction? And how do you achieve that without engaging yourself in this social engineering approach that would ultimately lead potentially to a lot more kids who aren't being raised by having a family member in the home?",
